6045417] [17592186432781 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cd4c20e9-b428-5717-aa65-b028512ceffa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432782 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432704 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432782 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432712 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432782 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432715 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432782 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432722 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432782 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186432742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432782 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432782 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432782 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186432742 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432782 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d41a4fb2-3b50-5439-ab7b-d9726d39fc08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432783 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432783 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85420" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432783 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432783 #edb/ref 1071 "Which digits, when inserted into the blank, make the number $35\\,284\\,43◻$ divisible by $6?$\n\nI. $2$\n\nII. $5$\n\nIII. $4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432783 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "072352b0-88b5-50de-9e91-38bf12444d03" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432783 #edb/ref 1074 "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$ Remember that to determine whether a number is divisible by $3,$ we check whether the sum of the digits is divisible by $3.$\n\nFor the given number, the sum of the digits is\n\n$$\n3+5+2+8+4+4+3+◻=29+◻.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each of the given digits we can substitute into the blank.\n\n- If we substitute $2$ into the blank, we get $35\\,284\\,432,$ which is divisible by $2.$ However, it is not divisible by $3$ because the sum of the digits is $29+2=31,$ which is not divisible by $3.$ So, the number is *not* divisible by $6$ either.\n\n- If we substitute $5$ into the blank, we get $35\\,284\\,435,$ which is not divisible by $2.$ Therefore, it is not divisible by $6$ either.\n\n- If we substitute $4$ into the blank, we get $35\\,284\\,434,$ which is divisible by $2.$ The sum of the digits is $29+4=33,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432784 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432784 #edb/ref 1069 "q-223458" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432784 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432784 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $7?$\n\nI. $276$\n\nII. $647$\n\nIII. $805$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432784 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1d92850b-bd66-52a9-a628-fb23ae6c0162" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432784 #edb/ref 1074 "To check if a number is divisible by $7,$ double the last digit, subtract the result from the remaining digits, and then check if the difference is divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number $276,$ we have that \n\n$$\n27-2(6)=27-12=15\n$$\n\nwhich is not divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $276$ is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number $647,$ we have that \n\n$$\n64-2(7)=64-14=50\n$$\n\nwhich is not divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $647$ is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number $805,$ we have that \n\n$$\n80-2(5)=80-10=70\n$$\n\nwhich is divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $805$ is divisible by $7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432785 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432785 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132405" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432785 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432785 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $6?$\n\nI. $185\\,346$\n\nII. $2\\,525\\,934$\n\nIII. $15\\,070\\,962$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432785 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "245a9f1f-3fc0-594c-a273-205b94f4bf02" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432785 #edb/ref 1074 "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$\n\n- The number $185\\,346$ is divisible by $2,$ and it is also divisible by $3$ since the sum of its digits is divisible by $3:$\n\n$$\n1+8+5+3+4+6=27\n$$\n\nSo, $185\\,346$ is divisible by $6.$\n\n- The number $2\\,525\\,934$ is divisible by $2,$ and it is also divisible by $3$ since the sum of its digits is divisible by $3:$\n\n$$\n2+5+2+5+9+3+4=30\n$$\n\nSo, $2\\,525\\,934$ is divisible by $6.$\n\n- The number $15\\,070\\,962$ is divisible by $2,$ and it is also divisible by $3$ since the sum of its digits is divisible by $3:$\n\n$$\n1+5+0+7+0+9+6+2=30\n$$\n\nSo, $15\\,070\\,962$ is divisible by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432786 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432786 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85424" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432786 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432786 #edb/ref 1071 "Which digits, when inserted into the blank, make the number $591\\,030\\,47◻$ divisible by $6?$\n\nI. $2$\n\nII. $5$\n\nIII. $4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432786 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "66ca27c6-8cac-5ac3-b80e-6fd1dcb7b6a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432786 #edb/ref 1074 "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$ Remember that to determine whether a number is divisible by $3,$ we check whether the sum of the digits is divisible by $3.$\n\nFor the given number, the sum of the digits is\n\n$$\n5+9+1+0+3+0+4+7+◻=29+◻.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each of the given digits we can substitute into the blank.\n\n- If we substitute $2$ into the blank, we get $591\\,030\\,472,$ which is divisible by $2.$ However, it is not divisible by $3$ because the sum of the digits is $29+2=31,$ which is not divisible by $3.$ So, the number is *not* divisible by $6$ either.\n\n- If we substitute $5$ into the blank, we get $591\\,030\\,475,$ which is not divisible by $2,$ so it is not divisible by $6$ either.\n\n- If we substitute $4$ into the blank, we get $591\\,030\\,474,$ which is divisible by $2.$ The sum of the digits is $29+4=33,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432787 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432787 #edb/ref 1069 "q-223454" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432787 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432787 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $7?$\n\nI. $434$\n\nII. $785$\n\nIII. $587$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432787 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6daa78eb-7b75-5f11-9d14-72a95f4ad0c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432787 #edb/ref 1074 "To check if a number is divisible by $7,$ double the last digit, subtract the result from the remaining digits, and then check if the difference is divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number $434,$ we have that \n\n$$\n43-2(4)=43-8=35\n$$\n\nwhich is divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $434$ is divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number $785,$ we have that \n\n$$\n78-2(5)=78-10=68\n$$\n\nwhich is not divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $785$ is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number $587,$ we have that \n\n$$\n58-2(7)=58-14=44\n$$\n\nwhich is not divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $587$ is not divisible by $7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432788 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432788 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432788 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432788 #edb/ref 1071 "Which digits, when inserted into the blank, make the number $173\\,◻32\\,338$ divisible by $9?$\n\nI. $4$\n\nII. $6$\n\nIII. $9$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432788 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7d8254ce-5d00-53a5-a718-295062329804" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432788 #edb/ref 1074 "A number is divisible by $9$ if the sum of its digits is divisible by $9.$\n\nFor the number $173\\,◻32\\,338\\,$ the sum of the digits is\n\n$$\n1+7+3+◻+3+2+3+3+8=30+◻.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each of the given digits that could be inserted into the blank.\n\n- If we insert $4$ into the blank, then the sum of the digits is $30+4=34,$ which is *not* divisible by $9.$\n\n- If we insert $6$ into the blank, then the sum of the digits is $30+6=36,$ which is divisible by $9.$\n\n- If we insert $9$ into the blank, then the sum of the digits is $30+9=39,$ which is *not* divisible by $9.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432789 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432789 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132408" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432789 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432789 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $6?$\n\nI. $345\\,989\\,191$\n\nII. $55\\,355\\,676$\n\nIII. $137\\,252\\,124$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432789 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "91c8241d-8043-5a59-abeb-68e020936dc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432789 #edb/ref 1074 "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$\n\n- The number $345\\,989\\,191$ is not divisible by $2,$ so it is not divisible by $6.$\n\n- The number $55\\,355\\,676$ is divisible by $2,$ and it is also divisible by $3$ since the sum of its digits is divisible by $3:$\n\n$$\n5+5+3+5+5+6+7+6=42\n$$\n\nSo, $55\\,355\\,676$ is divisible by $6.$\n\n- The number $137\\,252\\,124$ is divisible by $2,$ and it is also divisible by $3$ since the sum of its digits is divisible by $3:$\n\n$$\n1+3+7+2+5+2+1+2+4=27\n$$\n\nSo, $137\\,252\\,124$ is divisible by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432790 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432790 #edb/ref 1069 "q-223369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432790 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432790 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $7?$\n\nI. $447$\n\nII. $406$\n\nIII. $847$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432790 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "951452dc-b5fb-50d7-8d4f-8b1233b43cf0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432790 #edb/ref 1074 "To check if a number is divisible by $7,$ double the last digit, subtract the result from the remaining digits, and then check if the difference is divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number $447,$ we have that \n\n$$\n44-2(7)=44-14=30\n$$\n\nwhich is not divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $447$ is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number $406,$ we have that \n\n$$\n40-2(6)=40-12=28\n$$\n\nwhich is divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $406$ is divisible by $7.$\n\n- For the number $847,$ we have that \n\n$$\n84-2(7)=84-14=70\n$$\n\nwhich is divisible by $7.$ Therefore, $847$ is divisible by $7.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432791 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432791 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85422" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432791 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432791 #edb/ref 1071 "Which digits, when inserted into the blank, make the number $114\\,040\\,32◻$ divisible by $6?$\n\nI. $0$\n\nII. $5$\n\nIII. $6$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432791 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a49592c2-4649-5980-ab80-d5418f7e763f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432791 #edb/ref 1074 "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$ Remember that to determine whether a number is divisible by $3,$ we check whether the sum of the digits is divisible by $3.$\n\nFor the given number, the sum of the digits is\n\n$$\n1+1+4+0+4+0+3+2+◻=15+◻.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each of the given digits we can substitute into the blank.\n\n- If we substitute $0$ into the blank, we get $114\\,040\\,320,$ which is divisible by $2.$ The sum of the digits is $15+0=15,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\n- If we substitute $5$ into the blank, we get $114\\,040\\,325,$ which is not divisible by $2.$ So, the number is *not* divisible by $6$ either.\n\n- If we substitute $6$ into the blank, we get $114\\,040\\,326,$ which is divisible by $2.$ The sum of the digits is $15+6=21,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432792 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186432774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432792 #edb/ref 1069 "q-85393" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432792 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432792 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following numbers are divisible by $3?$\n\nI. $896\\,154$\n\nII. $12\\,471\\,106$\n\nIII. $3\\,472\\,335$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432792 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bf3c9279-64c9-519d-9697-63c8e9dc9b5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432792 #edb/ref 1074 "A number is divisible by $3$ if the sum of its digits is divisible by $3.$\n\n- The sum of the digits of $896\\,154$ is\n\n$$\n8+9+6+1+5+4=33,\n$$\n\nwhich is divisible by $3.$ So, $896\\,154$ is divisible by $3.$\n\n- The sum of the digits of $12\\,471\\,106$ is\n\n$$\n1+2+4+7+1+1+0+6=22,\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* divisible by $3.$ So, $12\\,471\\,106$ is *not* divisible by $3.$\n\n- The sum of the digits of $3\\,472\\,335$ is\n\n$$\n3+4+7+2+3+3+5=27,\n$$\n\nwhich is divisible by $3.$ So, $3\\,472\\,335$ is divisible by $3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17